# Notion MCP — read-only

When using the **Notion** MCP server, you may **only** invoke tools that read or query data. **Do not** call any tool that creates, updates, deletes, moves, duplicates, or comments on content.

## Allowed (read / query)

Per [Notion MCP supported tools](https://developers.notion.com/docs/mcp-supported-tools), these are read-only:

- `notion-search`
- `notion-fetch`
- `notion-query-data-sources`
- `notion-query-database-view`
- `notion-get-comments`
- `notion-get-teams`
- `notion-get-users`
- `notion-get-user`
- `notion-get-self`

## Forbidden (mutations)

Do **not** use:

- `notion-create-pages`, `notion-update-page`, `notion-move-pages`, `notion-duplicate-page`
- `notion-create-database`, `notion-update-data-source`
- `notion-create-view`, `notion-update-view`
- `notion-create-comment`

If the user asks for work that requires a forbidden tool, explain the policy and suggest they do the change in the Notion app, or ask them to temporarily relax this rule.

## Claude Code

Hard blocks for mutating tools live in **`~/.claude/settings.json`** → `permissions.deny`, using Claude Code’s pattern `mcp__<serverId>__<toolName>` (see [permissions — MCP](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions#mcp)). This repo assumes the Notion MCP server id is **`notion`**; if yours differs (check `/mcp` or MCP config), rename the middle segment in those rules (for example `mcp__my-notion__…`).

## Cursor settings

Cursor cannot fully block individual MCP tools via repo config alone; this rule is the behavioral guardrail. For **auto-run**, you can optionally set `~/.cursor/permissions.json` `mcpAllowlist` to only the allowed `notion:notion-*` pairs (verify the exact server key in **Cursor Settings → MCP** matches `notion`). Tool names may omit the `notion-` prefix in some clients—match what **Available Tools** shows.
